+Timing scores limit the quantity advantage. On Taxi, CNN+GRU54 records time NLL 1.4684, compared with 0.6516 for NHP. On Intermittent, the corresponding scores are 0.3025 for CNN+GRU54 and 0.2349 for S2P2. NHP also records lower time NLL on RAF, 3.3889 versus 3.5464. Each score comes from the same checkpoint as its row's quantity errors, so none substitutes an independently selected timing checkpoint.
+
+The nonlearned references provide a further check. CNN+GRU54 attains lower RMSE than both rules on Taxi and RAF. On Intermittent, repeating the last quantity yields lower MAE, 0.5647 versus 0.7022, and slightly lower RMSE, 1.7078 versus 1.7296. Thus the lower errors against learned encoder adaptations do not establish superiority over every simple predictor. Appendix~\ref{app:legacy-external} retains the numerical reference values, and Appendix~\ref{app:cnngru-records} reports all six CNN+GRU54 metrics, including the large-quantity subsets.
+
+\subsection{Additional effect of explicit history correction}
+The full, correction-removal, and current-only CNN+GRU54 comparison is under evaluation. The completed performance panel establishes the full model's position among the tested encoders, but cannot attribute that position to the correction path. Attribution requires the paired controls described in Section~4, including their large-quantity errors, timing scores, and computational costs.
+
+The earlier MLP8 study motivates this comparison. Its correction improved overall quantity RMSE in all 18 paired comparisons, with timing and large-quantity exceptions retained in Appendix~\ref{app:metrics}. That finding concerns the MLP path. It does not establish the effect of the local and recurrent paths in CNN+GRU54.
+
+\subsection{Additional effect of prototype retrieval}
+The matched CNN+GRU54 retrieval comparison is also under evaluation. It compares mean, weighted, fixed, and general-query retrieval, retaining the same encoder and output-head definitions. A fixed-query gain over mean alone would not identify the value of separating time and quantity queries, because weighting and general query learning remain alternative explanations. No completed three-dataset result for this matched comparison is reported here.
+
+\section{Discussion}
+The completed CNN+GRU54 results support a bounded performance claim. Under common numerical inputs and output heads, its mean quantity errors are lower than those of the six tested encoder adaptations on Taxi and Intermittent. RAF favors S2P2 by mean RMSE, and timing scores favor other encoders on all three datasets. The same representative architecture is retained throughout, without selecting a different TitanTPP variant for each dataset.
+
+These performance differences and the correction mechanism answer separate questions. CNN+GRU54 reads three local positions and carries a recurrent state across the observed prefix. A successful full-versus-current-only comparison would support that combined path, rather than a claim that one preceding state alone explains the gain. Equal active parameter counts narrow the capacity explanation but do not remove differences in operations, depth, or optimization. The earlier MLP8 findings in Appendix~\ref{app:metrics} motivate this experiment without replacing it.
+
+The retrieval hypothesis is narrower still. Fixed split-query scoring can be expressed as a concatenated dot product. A benefit over the matched general query would support the tested separate-normalization constraint, not the invention of a fundamentally new memory mechanism. Earlier exploratory observations informed this hypothesis, making the new comparison a development study rather than independent confirmation.
+
+The completed panel combines separately trained models and reused seed-42 CNN+GRU54 runs across hardware. Its agreement in task and selection rules permits a descriptive comparison, but does not establish matched initialization or hardware efficiency. All performance reported here comes from validation evidence used during research. An independently unexposed evaluation remains outstanding, and three-seed summaries do not establish statistical significance.
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+TitanTPP augments a causal attention encoder with local and recurrent history correction. Completed CNN+GRU54 runs attain lower mean quantity errors than six common-head encoder adaptations on Taxi and Intermittent, while S2P2 attains lower mean RMSE on RAF. Timing scores also qualify the quantity gains. The separate controls will test whether correction and retrieval changes provide additional gains, together with their timing and computational costs. Earlier MLP4 and MLP8 results remain supporting evidence for the research questions, with their full comparisons preserved in the appendices.
